  "Number": 18,
  "Question": "One of the factors of \\(x^4+4\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x^2+2\\)",
    "B": "\\(x+1\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^2-2x+2\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^2-4\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The square \\((x^2+2)^2\\) contains \\(x^4+4\\), but it also has the middle term \\(4x^2\\). Adding and subtracting that term reveals a difference of squares:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^4+4\n&=x^4+4x^2+4-4x^2\\\\\n&=(x^2+2)^2-(2x)^2\\\\\n&=(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last step uses \\(A^2-B^2=(A-B)(A+B)\\). This polynomial identity shows that \\(x^2-2x+2\\) is a factor, choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
